\textbf{Step 4: no shorter period.} The $m=1$ term alone has smallest period $P_2$, while the $m=2,3$ terms also repeat after $P_2/2$ and $P_2/3$; a shift that left the sum unchanged would have to leave this slowest component unchanged as well, which first happens at $P_2$.
\[
\boxed{P_2=2\pi/\log2\approx9.065\ \text{(period of RE2)},\quad P_3\approx5.719,\ P_5\approx3.904,\ P_7\approx3.229.}
\]
\end{sample}

Step 4 relied on intuition for the slowest component; Chapter~\ref{ch:fourier-series} makes it precise using orthogonality.

