\textbf{Step 4: what we have and have not shown.} Step 3 proves that $P_2$ is \emph{a} period of RE2. That no shorter shift leaves the sum unchanged is also true, but proving it requires separating the $m=1$ component from the others, which is done with the orthogonality of sines in Chapter~\ref{ch:fourier-series}; we do not use minimality here.
\[
\boxed{P_2=2\pi/\log2\approx9.065\ \text{(a period of RE2)},\quad P_3\approx5.719,\ P_5\approx3.904,\ P_7\approx3.229.}
\]
\end{sample}
